## Notes on M365 Copilot's run

- **Failure mode is "weak search," not truncation.** Unlike Cowork (which hit a hard `web_fetch` cap and refused cleanly with section citations), M365 Copilot returned 7 "Not located" responses for facts that *are* in the uploaded files — including the segment names (Q9, basic Item 1) which Cowork managed to get from a truncated fetch. So this isn't a tool-limit issue; M365 didn't search the attached files thoroughly.
- **Correctly handled both traps**: Q14 🪤 and Q15 🪤 both arrived at the right conclusion (no impairment / nation-state not a top-level category). Q14 was hedged as "could not find" rather than the cleaner "no impairment recorded", but the model didn't fabricate. Net: trap pass rate 2/2.
- **Rounding discipline**: Q1/Q2 reported "$245 billion" / "$109 billion" — both one digit rounder than the source ($245.1B / $109.4B). The prompt explicitly required "match the source exactly, including rounding" → ⚠️ for both.
- **What it got right**: Q3 (arithmetic), Q13 (Activision date), Q14 🪤, Q15 🪤. Plus partials on Q1, Q2, Q7, Q10, Q13.
- **Time**: ~60 s (marker mode — externally timed). M365 Copilot's Analyst persona / Python execution wasn't enabled in this run. Faster than Claude Code (236 s) and Cowork (151 s); slower than Claude.ai (39 s).
- **Comparison**: Worse than Cowork on effective score (2 ✅ vs 3 ✅) despite having the full files attached. M365 Copilot underperformed its information access here.

## Notes on NotebookLM's run

- **14/15 — strongest run overall on answer quality.** Got the exact SCT figure ($79,106,183) AND the Q13 verbatim Item 1C quote ("We also engage in robust cybersecurity assessments and remediation efforts for acquired companies") — the combination no other competitor managed. Claude.ai missed Q5 precision; ChatGPT missed Q13 verbatim; Claude Cowork hit truncation; M365 had weak search; NotebookLM did both.
- **Q7 ⚠️** — only miss. Did not name NIST CSF for the 10-K half of the question; only answered the proxy half (Board / ≥ quarterly). Likely read "Microsoft assesses its cybersecurity program against a national framework" as a factual prelude rather than an implicit sub-question. Defensible literal reading of the question, but the ground-truth rubric expected the framework to be named.
- **Q14 🪤 — cleanest answer of any competitor**: "The provided documents do not contain any information stating that Microsoft recorded an impairment charge related to the Activision Blizzard acquisition." No hedge about "accessible text" or "available excerpts" — just the trap conclusion stated plainly.
- **Q15 🪤**: Named the correct parent category verbatim in all-caps as it appears in the filing ("CYBERSECURITY, DATA PRIVACY, AND PLATFORM ABUSE RISKS") AND the specific sub-section ("Security of our information technology") — same sub-section detail Claude.ai's v9 run cited. Didn't enumerate all 7 categories but matched the depth of the highest-scoring Claude answer.
- **Citation discipline**: Every answer included a precise page citation. Q1 included two cross-references (p. 43 + p. 56) for the same fact across different sections of the 10-K — the only competitor to do so.
- **Source ingestion**: NotebookLM ingested both SEC URLs natively (no `.htm` conversion needed), avoiding both Cowork's truncation problem and M365's upload-then-fail-to-search issue.
- **Time**: ~30 s (marker mode, externally timed). Fastest non-Normain competitor — beats Claude.ai (39 s), M365 Copilot (~60 s), Cowork (151 s), ChatGPT (158 s), Claude Code (236 s).
- **Pattern**: NotebookLM is the cleanest combination of precision + verbatim citation + trap discipline seen so far. The one ⚠️ is a question-interpretation issue, not a retrieval or reasoning failure.

## Notes on ChatGPT's run

- **14/15 — matches Claude.ai's score but with the Q5 win.** ChatGPT got the SCT figure exactly right ($79,106,183), the question Claude.ai missed by $1,551. With both files attached as Project sources, ChatGPT executed against the actual SCT row rather than relying on summary memory.
- **Q13 ⚠️** — only miss. Got the exact Oct 13 2023 acquisition date, but explicitly flagged that "the accessible SEC text did not provide a fuller exact quote" for the Item 1C language. So ChatGPT correctly *avoided* fabricating the quote but didn't extract it either. Same "verbatim-quote miss" pattern Cowork showed on Q13.
- **Q8**: Said "ISO 31000" without the `:2018` version specifier — kept as ✅ since the standard reference is correct; the year suffix is a minor precision loss not a different answer.
- **Q14 🪤**: Cleaner phrasing than M365's identical-spirit answer — explicitly notes "The filing discusses the acquisition and goodwill/intangible accounting, but the sources available here did not state a specific Activision-related impairment charge." That's the trap answer. ✅.
- **Q15 🪤**: Correctly answers the binary "no" + correctly places nation-state as a sub-issue; only names 2 of 7 top-level categories (Strategic & Competitive, Operational) as examples. Compare to Claude / Claude Code who listed all 7. The binary answer is right.
- **Time**: 158 s — self-measured via Python `time.time()`, the precise method. Slower than Claude.ai (39 s) and M365 (~60 s), comparable to Cowork (151 s), faster than Claude Code (236 s).
- **Pattern**: With Project file attachment + Code Interpreter, ChatGPT avoids both Cowork's retrieval-truncation failure mode AND Claude.ai's number-rounding failure mode. The remaining weakness is verbatim-quote extraction.

## Grading process

For each competitor:
1. Run all 15 questions in their tool with the same source URLs.
2. Time the run — paste-to-final-token.
3. Capture each answer (text + screenshot).
4. Fill the corresponding column with: brief answer summary + verdict emoji.
5. Update score summary including the time column.
6. Note any anomalies (citation fabrications, refusals, ingestion failures) in a per-competitor notes section.
